    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "In order to study cross joint geometries in a compressive stress environment effects of pore pressure are incorporated in the cross joint theory proposed by Bai and Gross (in review). This study demonstrates that cross joint geometries as a function of pore pressure may vary among 1) curving-parallel; 2) curving-perpendicular; 3) non-curving; 4) quasi-curving-parallel with compressive zone; and 5) quasi-curving-perpendicular with or without compressive zone. The specific variation among these categories depends upon the take off angle as well as the remote principal stress ratio. However, in all cases, connectivity between cross joints and pre-existing joints is improved with increasing pore pressure. Therefore, the general rule that can be drawn is: fluid conductivity in the direction normal to a pre-existing joint set increases as cross joints form with increasing pore pressure. These results provide a tool for predicting cross joint geometry given the knowledge of the remote principal stress ratio, the orientations of the maximum horizontal stress and the pre-existing joint set, as well as the magnitude of pore pressure.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
